<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Colchis</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 101 BCE (approx.) · capital Vani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The land of the Golden Fleece on the eastern Black Sea, an early Georgian kingdom whose gold, timber and Greek trading ports made it a bridge between the Caucasus and the Greek world.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colchis appears as Qulha in Urartian inscriptions of the 8th century BCE, when it emerged in the lowlands of the Rioni and its foothills, in part inheriting the lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/diauehi">Diauehi</a>. Greek colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> founded ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phasis-town">Phasis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dioscurias">Dioscurias</a> in the 6th century BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonaut</a> legend fixed its fame.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power was held by kings and local sceptre-bearing chiefs ruling from towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vani">Vani</a>, whose rich burials hold fine gold jewellery. Colchians traded linen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, honey and slaves with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, sent regular gifts to the Achaemenid court. They spoke a Kartvelian language, and local coins called Colchian tetri circulated widely.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 2nd century BCE the kingdom fragmented under local rulers, and about 101 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a> annexed it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus</a>. After Pontus fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the coast passed under Roman influence and the western Georgian kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lazica">Lazica</a> later grew in the same lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-colchis.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Colchis" width="320" height="281"><figcaption>Image: Deu, basiert auf Andrew Anderson's File:Earlycaucasus655.jpg und Don-Kun's File:Caucasus 300 map alt de.png · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgian_States_Colchis_and_Iberia_(600-150BC)-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 101 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Colchian paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Colchian (Kartvelian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colchis, Kolkha, Egrisi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=kingdom-of-colchis" aria-label="Kingdom of Colchis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5699 674 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Colchis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6019 930L6140 1251M6019 930L5879 982M6019 930L6023 927"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5879 982h0M6023 927h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6019 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6019 930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=kingdom-of-colchis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
